{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>Naphtali, sated with favor</b> — that his land was sated with every favor for its inhabitants; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 33:23). But the correct interpretation is that it is drawn toward the glorious Name: he says, sated and filled with the favor of the glorious Name and His blessing.\n\nBut in the midrash of Rabbi Nechunya ben HaKana, they said concerning this verse: Rabbi Amora said, what is written — <i>and full of the blessing of YHWH, sea and south shall he inherit</i>? Thus Moses said: if you walk in My statutes, you shall inherit the World to Come, which is likened to the sea, as it is written (Job 11:9) <i>and broader than the sea</i>; and this world, which is likened to the south, as it is written (Joshua 15:19) <i>for the land of the Negev you have given me</i>, and they translate it [in Aramaic] <i>ar'a deroma</i> (the land of the south). And what is written <i>yerashah</i> (he shall inherit)? It should have said <i>yarash</i> (he inherited)! Rather, even the Holy One, blessed be He, is included — and that is <i>Rash Yah</i> (the inheritance of YHWH). To what is the matter likened? To a king who had two treasuries and set aside one of them. After many days, he said to his son: take what is in these two treasuries. The son said: perhaps he will not give me what he set aside. He said to him: take everything. And that is what is written: <i>sea and south yerashah</i> — <i>Rash Yah</i>, the inheritance of YHWH — everything is given to you, if only you keep His ways.\n\nAnd they further said: <i>and full of the blessing of YHWH, sea and south shall he inherit</i> — there is no \"sea\" but Torah, as it is written <i>and broader than the sea</i>. And so the entire midrash there.\n\nIf so, the secret of the verse will be: <b>Naphtali, sated with the divine favor</b> that fills <b>the blessing of YHWH</b> — which is the Sea included within all, from all, in the manner of what is written (Ecclesiastes 1:7) <i>All the streams go to the sea</i>. And it is the House of the King. As a parable: those who seek to see the King first ask, where is the House of the King? And afterward they ask, where is the King? And also this world, parched and dry from the divine favor, he shall inherit through <i>Yah</i>. And the enlightened will understand."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {0 "The phrase רָשׁ יָהּ (Rash Yah) is a transposition of יָהּ רָשׁ, reading 'the inheritance of YHWH'; the midrash plays on the word יְרָשָׁה (yerashah, 'he shall inherit') as though punctuated יָהּ רָשׁ."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:59:55.322109344Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}